  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   - We start from the provided parent solution (n=47) and perform local optimization to find any improvement.\n   - After optimization, if we don't find a better solution, we double the grid points (n=94) and repeat the optimization, as higher resolution might yield a better solution.\n   - We formalize the problem as minimizing the maximum correlation between h and (1-h), subject to h being a step function with integral 1.\n   - We use scipy.optimize.minimize with SLSQP method because it handles both box constraints and an equality constraint.\n   - If time permits, we double the grid and repeat until we either exceed the budget or find a candidate below the target 0.3808.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - Variables: h[i] (n-dimensional vector, length n_points)\n   - Objective function: \n        C_5 = max_k \u222b h(x) * (1-h(x+k)) dx = max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\") * (2.0/n_points))\n   - Constraints:\n        - Bound constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n        - Equality constraint: sum(h) = n_points/2 (must be exact for the integrated integral to be 1, given dx=2.0/n_points)\n   - Solving method: Local NLP with SLSQP, using the gradient to improve convergence.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   - Compute initial function value at parent solution (n=47)\n   - Set bounds and constraint function\n   - Use scipy.optimize.minimize with SLSQP method, starting from h.\n   - Apply a series of local optimizations as a penalty function for slightly perturbed parents\n   - If found a solution, compare to previous best\n   - If didn't improve and still within budget, double n_points and repeat the process\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n   - Base scenario: Start from initial solution and optimize at n=47\n   - If solution is below budget, try n=94\n   - For each grid size: Run multiple local optimizations with different starting points\n   - Time budget per optimization: Fraction of total budget, decreasing with iterations\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   - 1100 seconds allocated\n   - Primary optimization at n=47 estimated to take <10% of budget\n   - Successive doublings have higher dimension and take ~3x the previous optimization time each\n   - Multiple optimization calls should be budgeted with a time monitor\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Ensure 0<=h[i]<=1 and sum(h)==n_points/2\n   - Return only feasible candidates with evaluated C_5\n   - When doubling grid size, use linear interpolation from parent solution to maintain feasibility\n   - If time runs out, return best candidate found\n   - Termination when max bound attempts, grid size doubled beyond 47*2^3=376 (if time allows)",
